Appendix B: Photovoltaic Cell Technology and Radiation Hardness Data

B.1 IMM3J Cell Architecture and Performance

The inverted metamorphic triple-junction (IMM3J) solar cell, consisting of GaInP (top), GaAs (middle), and InGaAs (bottom) subcells, achieves AM0 efficiencies exceeding 32% at 28°C in large-area formats. This efficiency represents the state of the art for flexible thin-film space solar cells and forms the basis for the BOL specific power calculations in Section 5.1.

The metamorphic growth approach enables the lattice-mismatched integration of the 1.0 eV InGaAs bottom subcell, which extends the spectral response into the near-infrared and significantly improves overall conversion efficiency compared to lattice-matched triple-junction designs. However, as demonstrated in Section 2.1, this efficiency gain comes at the cost of increased radiation sensitivity.

B.2 Proton Irradiation Degradation Characteristics

Recent high-energy proton irradiation studies on flexible thin-film IMM3J solar cells provide the empirical foundation for the NIEL-correlated degradation model presented in Section 2.1. Cells were irradiated with 1, 3, and 5 MeV protons, with SRIM simulations confirming that these energies produce uniform displacement damage throughout the active layers of the device.

Key experimental findings include:

· The open-circuit voltage ($V_{oc}$) and maximum power ($P_{max}$) exhibit logarithmic degradation with increasing displacement damage dose (DDD).
· The InGaAs bottom subcell is the current-limiting junction under all proton energies tested, with its degradation rate exceeding that of the GaInP top cell by a factor of approximately 2.3.
· When converted to DDD via NIEL scaling, the degradation of $P_{max}$ for 1, 3, and 5 MeV protons collapses onto a single curve, validating the use of NIEL as a unifying damage metric.

B.3 Electron Irradiation Degradation Factors

Degradation factors after exposure to 1 MeV electron irradiation have been characterized for both IMM3J and IMM4J technologies. The remaining factor of $P_{max}$ after $3.16 \times 10^{10}$ MeV/g displacement damage dose is approximately 0.86 for 1 MeV electrons, consistent with the degradation coefficients used in the optimal overbuild factor derivation of Section 2.5.

Comparative studies on degradation characteristics of component subcells in IMM triple-junction solar cells irradiated with high-energy electrons and protons confirm that the InGaAs subcell exhibits the most severe degradation across all particle types and energies. This differential degradation justifies the bottom-cell overbuild factor $\beta^* \approx 1.1$ derived in Theorem 5 of Section 2.5.

B.4 Displacement Damage Dose (DDD) Methodology

The use of Displacement Damage Dose (DDD) as a unifying metric for correlating damage from different particle types and energies is well established in the radiation effects community. DDD is calculated as the product of particle fluence and the appropriate NIEL value for the given particle energy and target material. The methodology enables the collapse of degradation data from electrons and protons of various energies onto a single characteristic curve.

For the IMM3J cell architecture, the NIEL values for 1 MeV electrons, 1 MeV protons, 3 MeV protons, and 5 MeV protons have been calculated using SRIM simulations. The logarithmic relationship between $P_{max}$ and DDD provides the analytical basis for the end-of-life efficiency predictions presented in Section 5.1 (EOL remaining factor > 0.82 at $1 \times 10^{15}$ e/cm² equivalent fluence).

