Appendix C: Hypervelocity Impact Protection Methodology

C.1 The BUMPER-II Risk Assessment Framework

NASA's BUMPER-II program is the authoritative tool for evaluating the risk of mission failure due to micrometeoroid and orbital debris (MMOD) impacts. The program combines debris and meteoroid environment models with finite element models of spacecraft geometry and predictive damage equations known as Ballistic Limit Equations (BLEs). BUMPER-II has been extensively used for risk assessment on the Space Shuttle Orbiter and the International Space Station.

The program provides point estimate predictions of MMOD risk, although it does not natively provide uncertainty bounds or confidence intervals for its predictions. The Monte Carlo framework developed in Section 4 addresses this limitation by incorporating importance sampling and explicit uncertainty propagation from the MASTER-8 flux models.

C.2 Cour-Palais/Christiansen Ballistic Limit Equations

The Cour-Palais/Christiansen equations are the most widely accepted ballistic limit equations for double-wall MMOD shielding configurations. For hypervelocity impacts ($v_n > 7$ km/s), the critical particle diameter is given by the Christiansen equation as presented in Eq. (4.8) of the main text. This equation has been empirically validated through thousands of hypervelocity impact tests at facilities including the Ernst-Mach-Institut (EMI) and the NASA Johnson Space Center Hypervelocity Impact Technology Facility.

The original equations have been modified to incorporate the influence of shield thickness more accurately, particularly for configurations with very thin bumpers. The modified equations consider the ratio of shield thickness to particle diameter as an additional parameter, improving accuracy in the hypervelocity region for lightweight shielding configurations similar to the Al/Nextel/CFRP sandwich proposed in Section 3.1.

C.3 Shield Material Properties

The graded-Z shielding configuration specified in Section 3.1 consists of three functional layers:

Bumper Layer (Al 6061-T6, 0.20 mm): Disrupts the incident projectile into a debris cloud, distributing the impact energy over a larger area of the rear wall. Aluminum 6061-T6 has a yield strength $\sigma_w \approx 40$ ksi and density $\rho = 2.7$ g/cm³. The bumper thickness is selected to optimize the trade between projectile disruption and mass penalty.

Fragment Dispersion Layer (Nextel 312 AF-10, 0.05 mm): A ceramic fabric that further disperses the debris cloud fragments and provides thermal blanketing. Nextel 312 has a density of approximately 2.7 g/cm³ and maintains mechanical integrity at temperatures up to 1200°C, making it suitable for both MMOD protection and thermal management.

Rear Wall (CFRP M55J/cyanate ester, 0.10 mm): Provides structural rigidity and serves as the final barrier against any penetrating fragments. The carbon fiber reinforced polymer exhibits high specific stiffness and anisotropic thermal conductivity, with $k_{in-plane} \approx 50$ W/m·K and $k_{through-thickness} \approx 1$ W/m·K. The CFRP rear wall also functions as the substrate for the embedded copper-water heat pipes specified in Section 3.3.

C.4 Standoff Distance Optimization

The standoff distance $S$ between the bumper and the rear wall is a critical parameter in shield performance. For the ROSA-derived deployment mechanism, the natural gap between the deployed blanket and the underlying support structure provides a standoff of approximately 2.0 cm. This standoff allows the debris cloud generated by projectile impact on the bumper to expand before striking the rear wall, significantly increasing the effective shield performance without adding mass. The standoff factor $(S/D_p)^{0.5}$ in the full Cour-Palais/Christiansen equation accounts for this effect, as discussed in Section 2.2.

