Appendix D: Space Standards and Regulatory Framework

D.1 ECSS-Q-ST-60 Series: EEE Component Assurance

The ECSS-Q-ST-60C standard defines the requirements for the assurance of Electrical, Electronic, and Electromechanical (EEE) components in space projects. The 2025 revision introduces significant updates, including phase-gated component approval processes aligned with ESA mission phases, an updated preferred source hierarchy clarifying when to transition from ESCC-qualified to industrial or COTS suppliers, and expanded Destructive Physical Analysis (DPA) requirements for non-qualified parts.

Of particular relevance to the proposed modular array architecture is the prohibition of components with >97% tin content due to whisker growth risk. The distributed MPPT converters and solid-state isolation fuses specified in Section 3.2 must be procured in compliance with these material restrictions.

D.2 ECSS-Q-ST-60-15: Radiation Hardness Assurance

The ECSS-Q-ST-60-15 standard outlines the requirements for Radiation Hardness Assurance (RHA) of EEE components in space projects. The standard addresses the three main radiation effects on electronic components: Total Ionizing Dose (TID), Displacement Damage or Total Non-Ionizing Dose (TNID), and Single Event Effects (SEE).

For the GaN-on-Si MMICs and 4-bit phase shifters specified in Section 3.1, the RHA program must demonstrate compliance with TID tolerance up to 100 krad(Si) and SEL immunity up to LET > 75 MeV·cm²/mg. The NIEL-correlated degradation model presented in Section 2.1 provides the analytical framework for demonstrating compliance with the TNID requirements for the photovoltaic cells.

D.3 ECSS-E-ST-20-08: Photovoltaic Assemblies and Components

The ECSS-E-ST-20-08 standard governs the qualification, coverglass bonding, and interconnection processes for photovoltaic assemblies in space applications. The IMM3J cell qualification, DC 93-500 adhesive bonding, and ITO grounding mesh specified in Section 3 must adhere to the thermal cycling, humidity, and UV exposure test protocols defined in this standard. Compliance ensures that the modular tiles can withstand the approximately 30,000 thermal cycles experienced during a 5-year LEO mission.

D.4 NASA SSP 51700-C: ISS Program Hardware Certification

NASA SSP 51700-C defines the safety and reliability requirements for externally mounted payloads on the International Space Station. While the proposed modular array is not necessarily an ISS payload, the standard provides a widely accepted benchmark for MMOD shielding adequacy, fault isolation, and plasma mitigation. The specifications presented in Section 5.1 have been validated against the requirements of this standard to ensure a conservative, flight-ready design.

D.5 ITU-R P.676: Atmospheric Attenuation for WPT

ITU-R Recommendation P.676 provides the methodology for calculating attenuation by atmospheric gases in the frequency range 1–350 GHz. The current version, P.676-13, approved in August 2022, includes updated models for oxygen and water vapor absorption.

At the 2.45 GHz ISM band selected for the WPT system, gaseous attenuation is dominated by oxygen absorption, with negligible water vapor contribution under clear-sky conditions. The zenith-path attenuation value $\eta_{atm} = 0.982$ used in the end-to-end efficiency calculation in Section 5.1 is derived from the P.676 model for a 400 km slant path at 2.45 GHz. This value represents the product of oxygen attenuation (approximately 0.015 dB) and negligible water vapor attenuation for typical atmospheric conditions.

D.6 IEEE 1652-2018: Radiation Hardness Assurance of Electronic Components

IEEE 1652-2018 provides the standard methodology for radiation hardness assurance of electronic components for space applications. The standard defines the requirements for part categorization, radiation test planning, and statistical analysis of radiation degradation data. The NIEL-correlated degradation model and forward-bias annealing specification presented in Sections 2.1 and 3.4 provide the analytical basis for end-of-life performance prediction required by this standard.

D.7 NASA HDBK 8719.14: Limiting Orbital Debris

The NASA Handbook for Limiting Orbital Debris provides guidelines and assessment procedures for limiting orbital debris generation. Section 4.6 of the handbook addresses MMOD shielding requirements, including probability of no penetration (PNP) limits, risk assessment processes, and the use of the BUMPER code for compliance demonstration. The modular survivability framework developed in Section 4 aligns with the methodology prescribed in this handbook, providing a validated approach for demonstrating compliance with orbital debris mitigation requirements.